# Forbidden-token ledger for release artifacts (docs/log/35 §35.7.5).
#
# Every line names one string that must never appear in anything we ship:
# a former employer's name, a real person's name, an internal repository or
# host. They were removed from this repository's whole history on 2026-08-01,
# and this ledger is what keeps them from coming back through a build.
#
# The strings themselves are deliberately absent. This repository is public, so
# writing them here would publish exactly what the gate exists to keep out.
# What is stored is enough to recognise a string and nothing else:
#
#   <sha256 of the canonical form>  <length in runes>  <Rabin-Karp value>  <id>
#
# Canonical form = letters/digits lowercased, every run of anything else
# collapsed to one space, ends trimmed. deploy/release/scan slides a window of
# each length over the same folding of every artifact byte, so a term is caught
# however it is spelled ("Foo Inc.", "foo-inc", "FooInc", "foo\n * Inc") and
# wherever it sits, including inside a longer word or a file name.
#
# To add a term (the term never touches a file or the process list):
#   printf '%s' 'the term' | go run ./deploy/release/scan \
#     --ledger /dev/null --add --id <id> >> deploy/release/forbidden.sha256
#
# ids: corp-* employer, name-* person, host-* internal host or deployment domain,
#      repo-* internal repo, acct-* cloud account id, zone-* DNS hosted zone id
#
# A person goes in as the FULL name, one entry per word order — never the given
# name or the surname on its own. Measured on the real shipping set: a bare
# common Japanese name matched upstream credits in four unrelated places (vim's
# changelog, the git-lfs binary, yarn's bundled cli.js, a Debian copyright file).
# Exempting those by path would be a standing maintenance tax, since every base
# image bump can add another. Full names carry the signal without the noise; the
# cost is that a lone surname would not be caught, which is a fair trade.
